      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251848/assets/9e3a9bc6a2c642697cfa2b6525e3abf42a0227f0ac66eecade156a842fb5e92d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $u$ and $z.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u$ varies directly with $z,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nu=kz.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $z=-10$ and $u=2.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =k\\cdot (-10) \\\\ -\\frac{2}{10} & =k \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nu=-\\frac{1}{5}z.\n$$",
